{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>דָּן יָדִין עַמּוֹ כְּאַחַד שִׁבְטֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל.</b> He says that even though in the standards Judah journeys first and Dan last, nevertheless he equates him too — that he shall judge his people as one singled out among the tribes of Israel, namely Judah; for Samson judged Israel in his generation as David did in his generation, and just as it is written of the King Messiah who will come from Judah, ״וּבְרוּחַ שְׂפָתָיו יָמִית רָשָׁע״ (Isaiah 11:4), so too Dan shall be ״נָחָשׁ עֲלֵי דֶרֶךְ״ (Genesis 49:17), for every snake kills with the breath of its lips. And from the fact that it says ״יָדִין עַמּוֹ,״ infer from this that he is speaking of the wicked of Israel, whom he judges and consumes the thorns from the vineyard of YHWH of Hosts. And therefore it was necessary that Judah journey first and Dan last, because Judah is likened to a lion, whose main strength is in its tail; if so, when Judah goes before the camp of Israel and his rear is toward the people, it is as though his tail faces the camp of Israel — a hint that through him he judges his people to break lofty arms; and Dan is likened to a snake, whose strength is only in its mouth; therefore it is fitting that he journey last and his mouth face the camp of Israel, for through him he judges his people. And since every judge has many who rise against him, therefore it says ״לֹא תָגוּרוּ מִפְּנֵי אִישׁ״ (Deuteronomy 1:17), ״כִּי לִישׁוּעָתְךָ קִוִּיתִי ה׳״ (Genesis 49:18), and ״אֱלֹהִים נִצָּב בַּעֲדַת אֵל״ (Psalms 82:1) to deliver them from men of arm. And there are those who say that Dan would not flatter his own tribe, but Dan was as one of the tribes of Israel like the other tribes, for near and far were equal in his eyes." "And what we find — that Moses likened Dan to a lion's cub and Jacob likened him to a snake — is a hint that every judge has war both before and behind. There are those who brazen it out to his face, saying to him, 'I am adding judges against you,' as it is written (Deuteronomy 1:12), ״טָרְחֲכֶם וּמַשַּׂאֲכֶם וְרִיבְכֶם.״ And there are those who murmur behind him but not to his face. Therefore he must be possessed of strength and courage to do battle both before and behind, and not set his heart to all the things that people will say whether to his face or behind his back, but he must be as a lion and as a snake — this one's strength behind him and that one's strength before him. And this is a correct hint."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {0 "The lemma cites Genesis 49:16; verse addresses within the body that are already present in the Hebrew have been normalized in translation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:34:34.119681650Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}